129	<section id="s1" class="part">
130	<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
131	<p>You read items 28 and 29 at 06:42 UTC on 7 November 2026, so they are not on the page.</p>
132	<p>Two items, 30 and 31, changes to the coordinator skill that you have not read. Each shows its change against the text before it.</p>
133	<p>In the item, the words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on the item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>
134	
135	<section id="l30" class="part item">
136	<h3><span class="num">Change 30 &middot; batch.md, &ldquo;The go&rdquo;</span>What a batch record takes</h3>
137	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; underlined, the words that came in. No words of that text went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
138	<p class="where-in">references/batch.md, &ldquo;The go&rdquo;, a new bullet, line 38</p>
139	<ul><li><span class="add">Put into a batch record every piece of work on PLAN&rsquo;s road that moves the project forward and is well defined: its decisions are taken. A new deliverable still waits for its go (<code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;The curator&rsquo;s role and decisions&rdquo;). A piece that is not well defined waits. A well-defined piece waits only if it would put the batch&rsquo;s merge at risk. Two rungs put it at risk if the gather cannot merge their edits of one file or one declaration cleanly. A pair that merges cleanly but changes one behaviour two ways, or where one needs the other&rsquo;s result, goes in one batch only if one lands only with the other (the manifest&rsquo;s <code>landsOnlyWith</code>).</span></li></ul>
140	</div></div>
141	<div class="note"><p><strong>A new rule.</strong> The skill said when a batch is launched, but not which work its record takes. Between 05:20 and 05:30 UTC on 7 November, when the two code-generator rungs, <code>asif</code> and the trait table, joined climb batch 27, you stated the rule in chat: &ldquo;if they were well defined and not in conflict, why not include all work that moves us forward in a batch? But if there would be a risk to merge, I&rsquo;d delay it to deconflict a batch landing.&rdquo;</p>
142	<p>The text now says which work a batch takes and what puts the merge at risk. A batch record takes every piece of work on the plan&rsquo;s road that moves the project forward and is well defined, which means that its decisions are taken. A new deliverable still waits for your go, and a piece that is not well defined waits. A well-defined piece waits only if it would put the merge at risk. Two rungs put the merge at risk when the gather cannot cleanly merge their edits of one file or one declaration. Two rungs that merge cleanly but change one behaviour in two ways, or where one needs the other&rsquo;s result, go in one batch only if the manifest ties one to land only with the other.</p></div>
143	</section>
144	
145	<section id="l31" class="part item">
146	<h3><span class="num">Change 31 &middot; batch.md, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, and SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;</span>While a batch runs, the main tree stays open until the gather starts</h3>
147	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; struck through, the words that went; underlined, the words that came in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
148	<p class="where-in">references/batch.md, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, the second bullet, line 78</p>
149	<ul><li><span class="cut">From the launch until the run ends, by landing, by stopping or by holding its push for the curator, edit no tracked file in the main tree, the boot note included. The</span> <span class="add">The rungs, their skeptics and their judges work in their own worktrees, and do not read the main tree. The gather and the stages after it work there, and the</span> gather needs <code>git status --porcelain</code> <span class="cut">there</span> to print nothing. <span class="add">So until the gather starts, commit in the main tree as usual, by named paths, and push at once. From the gather&rsquo;s start until the batch&rsquo;s push, change nothing there.</span> Keep <code>origin/main</code> at or behind the main tree&rsquo;s head, or git refuses the commit stage&rsquo;s push. So push <code>main</code> from no other tree. From the main tree, push nothing that the batch committed: the commit stage pushes it.</li></ul>
150	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;, the bullet on committing, line 234, its last sentence</p>
151	<ul><li>Commit only the paths that you name. Commit a tracked file with <code>git commit -- &lt;paths&gt;</code>, which is the same as <code>git commit --only &lt;paths&gt;</code>. For a new file, first run <code>git add -- &lt;paths&gt;</code>, with the paths named, then commit it in the same way. Never use <code>git add -A</code>, <code>git add .</code> or <code>git commit -a</code>, and never commit without paths: each takes changes that are not yours. The main tree is the checkout at <code>/home/user/fortress</code>, not a worktree, and a batch&rsquo;s gather stages its files there. While a batch runs, <span class="cut">edit nothing tracked there (<code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;).</span> <span class="add">change the main tree only as <code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, allows.</span></li></ul>
152	</div></div>
153	<div class="note"><p><strong>A rule&rsquo;s meaning changed.</strong> The old text forbade any edit of a tracked file in the main tree, the boot note included, from a batch&rsquo;s launch to its landing. But the rungs, their skeptics and their judges work in their own worktrees, and only the gather and the stages after it work in the main tree, as you said in chat at 07:1x UTC on 7 November.</p>
154	<p>The text now lets the coordinator commit in the main tree as usual until the gather starts, by named paths and pushed at once. From the gather&rsquo;s start until the batch&rsquo;s push, the coordinator changes nothing there. The sentence in <code>SKILL.md</code> now points to this bullet in place of the old ban.</p></div>
155	</section>
156	
157	<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
158	<ul>
159	<li>Item 30: commits of 7 November, <code>9cb72aca5</code>, 07:21 UTC, &ldquo;skills: what a batch takes; the main tree while a batch runs&rdquo;, and <code>8937a5af0</code>, 07:31 UTC, &ldquo;skills: the cold read&rsquo;s fixes on what a batch takes and the main tree while a batch runs&rdquo;. Shown as the net change of the two to <code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;The go&rdquo;, against the text at <code>ad55f5f00</code>. The same commits also add the rule&rsquo;s sources to <code>references/sources.md</code>, which this page does not show. Your words in chat at 05:2x UTC on 7 November, when the two code-generator rungs, <code>asif</code> (gap-ledger row 818) and the trait table (row 733), joined climb batch 27: &ldquo;if they were well defined and not in conflict, why not include all work that moves us forward in a batch? But if there would be a risk to merge, I&rsquo;d delay it to deconflict a batch landing.&rdquo;</li>
160	<li>Item 31: commits of 7 November, <code>9cb72aca5</code> and <code>8937a5af0</code>, as for item 30, <code>66bac4fd9</code>, 07:32 UTC, and <code>bd7efb95b</code>, 10:39 UTC. Shown as the net change of the four to <code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, and to <code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;, against the text at <code>ad55f5f00</code>. The same commits also add the rule&rsquo;s sources to <code>references/sources.md</code>, which this page does not show. The batch script: <code>explorations/coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.js</code>, <code>MAIN_TREE_ROLE</code> and the roles of the gather and the commit stage. Your word in chat at 07:1x UTC on 7 November: the skills were edited during batch runs before, and only the gather is sensitive.</li>
161	</ul>
162	</div>
163	</section>
164	
165	<section id="s2" class="part">
166	<h2>Proposed changes</h2>
167	<p>Two drafts wait for your yes to their exact words: drafts 32 and 33. Each shows its new text whole, with the added words underlined, and its current text collapsed.</p>
168	
169	<section id="d32" class="part item">
170	<h3><span class="num">Draft 32 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;, the bullet on the held list, line 233</span>The held list read before every writer task with its own cold read</h3>
171	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">Proposed text, not landed; underlined, the words that come in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
172	<ul><li>Put on the held list each rule that the curator states about your work, and each change to how you work that you and the curator agree in conversation. Have the skill writer (<code>references/skill-rewrite.md</code>) put it into its skill at the next opportunity, as &ldquo;The curator&rsquo;s role and decisions&rdquo; says. <span class="add">That opportunity is the next task that you give the writer that has its own cold read, not a task that only fixes a cold read&rsquo;s flags. Before you give that task, read the held list, and add to the task each point that names the skill writer. Leave out a point of a reading that the curator has not said is done, and a point for <code>fortress-repo</code> while the record-landing workflow runs.</span> The curator&rsquo;s rule in conversation is not a yes to the skill&rsquo;s wording. Keep it on the held list until <span class="add">its skill holds it</span>. If a compaction comes <span class="add">before that</span>, quote the held rule into the boot note, so that the session after the boot works that way.</li></ul>
173	</div></div>
174	<details class="prior"><summary>Current text (tap to open)</summary><div>
175	<ul><li>Put on the held list each rule that the curator states about your work, and each change to how you work that you and the curator agree in conversation. Have the skill writer (<code>references/skill-rewrite.md</code>) put it into its skill at the next opportunity, as &ldquo;The curator&rsquo;s role and decisions&rdquo; says. The curator&rsquo;s rule in conversation is not a yes to the skill&rsquo;s wording. Keep it on the held list until <span class="cut">then</span>. If a compaction comes <span class="cut">first</span>, quote the held rule into the boot note, so that the session after the boot works that way.</li></ul>
176	</div></details>
177	<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes.</strong> &ldquo;At the next opportunity&rdquo; now names a moment: the next task that the coordinator gives the skill writer that has its own cold read. A task that only fixes a cold read&rsquo;s flags does not count, since it gets no cold read of its own. Before that task, the coordinator reads the held list and adds to the task every point for the skill writer. It leaves out the points of a reading that you have not yet said is done, since the reading keeps its points until its end. It also leaves out a point for <code>fortress-repo</code> while the record-landing workflow runs, since that workflow changes <code>fortress-repo</code> then. &ldquo;Until then&rdquo; and &ldquo;first&rdquo; now name the moment when the skill holds the point.</p>
178	<p><strong>Why.</strong> Before the compaction, the coordinator gave the skill writer your rule of 05:5x UTC, that the first batch landing after a light boot reads FACTS and POSITIONS whole. It left your rule of 05:2x UTC on what a batch takes on the held list, because no rule made it read the held list first. That rule has since landed, as item 30. Between 07:10 and 07:20 UTC you asked that &ldquo;a curator triggered update of skills should write a proposal of all skill updates from the held list as well&rdquo;.</p>
179	<p>The draft is wider than your words, and that part is the coordinator&rsquo;s proposal. It sweeps the held list before every task of the skill writer that has its own cold read, not only before one that you start, since every such task is a chance to bring a held rule into its skill, and the cost is small. It waits for your yes because it touches how the points of the held list reach the skills.</p></div>
180	</section>
181	
182	<section id="d33" class="part item">
183	<h3><span class="num">Draft 33 &middot; batch.md, &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, the paragraph on a held push, line 87, and step 3, line 91</span>A held push goes out once the review finds nothing against it</h3>
184	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">Proposed text, not landed; underlined, the words that come in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
185	<p class="where-in">references/batch.md, &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, the paragraph on a held push, line 87</p>
186	<p><span class="add">The batch workflow holds the push when a point to report still acts against a decision on record after the repairs. Its result then gives <code>pushHeld</code> true, and <code>heldBy</code> lists the points.</span> If the batch's push is held for the curator, take steps 1 to 3 at once on the local commits. Only these wait: the push, the parts of step 3's routing check that need the pushed hashes, and step 4, since the record-landing workflow pushes <code>main</code> itself. <span class="add">If <code>heldBy</code> names either of these, push only after the curator's go:</span></p>
187	<ul>
188	<li><span class="add">a step that cannot be undone;</span></li>
