<section id="l30" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 30 &middot; batch.md, &ldquo;The go&rdquo;</span>What a batch record takes</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; underlined, the words that came in. No words of that text went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">references/batch.md, &ldquo;The go&rdquo;, a new bullet, line 38</p>
<ul><li><span class="add">Put into a batch record every piece of work on PLAN&rsquo;s road that moves the project forward and is well defined: its decisions are taken. A new deliverable still waits for its go (<code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;The curator&rsquo;s role and decisions&rdquo;). A piece that is not well defined waits. A well-defined piece waits only if it would put the batch&rsquo;s merge at risk. Two rungs put it at risk if the gather cannot merge their edits of one file or one declaration cleanly. A pair that merges cleanly but changes one behaviour two ways, or where one needs the other&rsquo;s result, goes in one batch only if one lands only with the other (the manifest&rsquo;s <code>landsOnlyWith</code>).</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>A new rule.</strong> The skill said when a batch is launched, but not which work its record takes. Between 05:20 and 05:30 UTC on 7 November, when the two code-generator rungs, <code>asif</code> and the trait table, joined climb batch 27, you stated the rule in chat: &ldquo;if they were well defined and not in conflict, why not include all work that moves us forward in a batch? But if there would be a risk to merge, I&rsquo;d delay it to deconflict a batch landing.&rdquo;</p>
<p>The text now says which work a batch takes and what puts the merge at risk. A batch record takes every piece of work on the plan&rsquo;s road that moves the project forward and is well defined, which means that its decisions are taken. A new deliverable still waits for your go, and a piece that is not well defined waits. A well-defined piece waits only if it would put the merge at risk. Two rungs put the merge at risk when the gather cannot cleanly merge their edits of one file or one declaration. Two rungs that merge cleanly but change one behaviour in two ways, or where one needs the other&rsquo;s result, go in one batch only if the manifest ties one to land only with the other.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l31" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 31 &middot; batch.md, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, and SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;</span>While a batch runs, the main tree stays open until the gather starts</h3>
